#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "f37e7ffd-df2d-51a4-9eee-e53589011f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21744" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}$.\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot ((1.99 \\cdot 10^{30}) \\cdot (6.42 \\cdot 10^{23}))/((2.28 \\cdot 10^{11})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (6.67 \\cdot 1.99 \\cdot 6.42)/((2.28)^{2}) \\cdot (10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{30} \\cdot 10^{23})/((10^{11})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (16.3925…) \\cdot 10^{20} \\\\\n&= (1.63925…) \\cdot 10^{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/id #uuid "2ef6ba7e-4f1d-51d2-af64-e1a77a7d748f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21745" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}$. If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg}$, then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{r^{2}} \\\\\nFr^{2} &= Gm_{1}m_{2} \\\\\nm_{2} &= \\frac{Fr^{2}}{Gm_{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{2} &= ((3.0 \\cdot 10^{-4}) \\cdot (3.0 \\cdot 10^{3})^{2})/((6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot (2.0 \\cdot 10^{5})) \\\\\n&= (3.0 \\cdot (3.0)^{2})/(6.67 \\cdot 2.0) \\cdot (10^{-4} \\cdot (10^{3})^{2})/(10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= (2.023…) \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "e7043d87-7f25-55a3-bd36-49d4ba1ae3fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21746" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}$. The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{r^{2}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{F} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{F}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot ((5.97 \\cdot 10^{24}) \\cdot (7.35 \\cdot 10^{22}))/(1.98 \\cdot 10^{20}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6.67 \\cdot 5.97 \\cdot 7.35}{1.98} \\cdot \\frac{10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{24} \\cdot 10^{22}}{10^{20}} \\\\\n&= (147.816…) \\cdot 10^{15} \\\\\n&= (14.7816…) \\cdot 10^{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{14.7816… \\cdot 10^{16}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{14.7816…} \\cdot \\sqrt{10^{16}} \\\\\n&= (3.8446…) \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}$."}]}
